1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,505
Valet, live: A fresh macOS machine, and a real website: Yahoo Finance. No account, no login.

2
00:00:03,505 --> 00:00:15,461
1 · Install: Install Valet from its disk image

3
00:00:15,461 --> 00:00:22,823
2 · Set up: First launch (with a debugging port only this test build accepts, so the script can drive it)

4
00:00:22,823 --> 00:00:33,516
2 · Set up: Setup walks through the shop adapter first. This run skips it: it is about a real site

5
00:00:33,516 --> 00:00:42,834
2 · Set up: Connect a chat app. Valet gives it a config that starts its own bridge, with no token in it

6
00:00:42,834 --> 00:00:50,228
3 · Approve: Now the Yahoo Finance adapter. Its tools stay hidden from every client until it is approved

7
00:00:50,228 --> 00:00:57,795
3 · Approve: The review: it reaches finance.yahoo.com and its API host only, reads only, and asks for no login

8
00:00:57,795 --> 00:01:04,607
3 · Approve: And Wikipedia, approved the same way

9
00:01:04,607 --> 00:01:11,834
3 · Approve: Wikipedia: en.wikipedia.org only, three read-only APIs, no login

10
00:01:11,834 --> 00:01:28,781
4 · Chat: A chat app: a scripted stand-in for Claude that speaks real MCP

11
00:01:28,781 --> 00:01:38,389
4 · Chat: Connected through Valet’s bridge: 6 tools

12
00:01:38,389 --> 00:01:43,790
4 · Chat: The agent searches for a symbol. Valet fetches it live from Yahoo Finance

13
00:01:43,790 --> 00:01:59,863
4 · Chat: Then quotes: each is the stock’s page on finance.yahoo.com, read by Valet’s own browser

14
00:01:59,863 --> 00:02:09,282
4 · Chat: Valet spaces its requests to the site a few seconds apart, so this is unhurried on purpose

15
00:02:09,282 --> 00:02:19,289
4 · Chat: A follow-up the agent answers from Wikipedia, read live through Valet

16
00:02:19,289 --> 00:02:28,388
5 · Valet: Back in Valet: every call is in its audited activity log

17
00:02:28,388 --> 00:02:32,200
Valet, live: End of the run
